     "up": {"title":"Why do standards matter? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A secondary school in Dominica must buy 30 laptops and a new wireless router from different suppliers. The principal asks why the IT teacher insists that every device supports IEEE 802.11ax (Wi-Fi 6). Which reason is MOST convincing?","options":[{"t":"BCS accreditation requires schools to use IEEE-approved equipment in computer labs.","fb":"BCS accredits university degrees. It does not set Wi-Fi standards or rules for school equipment."},{"t":"Only IEEE members are allowed to sell laptops in the Caribbean.","fb":"This is false. IEEE membership is voluntary and has nothing to do with who may sell equipment."},{"t":"IEEE standards guarantee that the laptops cannot be hacked.","fb":"Standards define how devices communicate. They do not guarantee security, so the school still needs passwords and updates."},{"t":"Devices that follow the same IEEE standard interoperate, so laptops from any supplier will connect to the router and the school is not locked in to one brand.","fb":"Correct. Interoperability gives the school freedom to buy competitively and still have a working network.","ok":true}]}
