     "up": {"title":"Describe the field of IT (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured response: scope of IT and professional organisations, Grade One extension (15 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Define</b> Information Technology, making clear both the technology involved and its purpose. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Describe</b> TWO ways IT is used at the Bayview Hotel and <b>explain</b> the benefit of each to the business. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Discuss</b> TWO limitations of IT that affect the hotel, using the stimulus for at least one, and <b>suggest</b> how EACH could be reduced. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>The hotel's IT officer can afford to join only ONE professional organisation. <b>Compare</b> IEEE and BCS and <b>recommend</b> the more useful one for her role, with a justification. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":250,"max":500,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Information Technology is the use of ... to ... Its purpose is ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, IT is used to ... This benefits the hotel because ... Second, ... which means ...<br><b>(c)</b> One limitation is ... At the Bayview Hotel this meant ... It could be reduced by ... A second limitation is ... This could be reduced by ...<br><b>(d)</b> IEEE (Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers) focuses on ..., whereas BCS (The Chartered Institute for IT) focuses on ... For the IT officer, I recommend ... because ...</p>","checklist":["My definition separates the technology from its purpose.","I described two specific usages and explained a business benefit for each.","I discussed two different limitations, one taken from the power cut in the stimulus.","I suggested a realistic way to reduce EACH limitation.","I compared IEEE and BCS using matched points (focus and activities).","My recommendation is justified by the IT officer's actual role."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"(a) Definition: technology and purpose","marks":2,"look":["comput|hardware|software|network|telecom","information|data","purpose|need|organi[sz]ation|people|user"],"min":2,"strong":"Your definition covers the technology and its purpose.","weak":"Name the technologies (computers, software, networks, telecommunications) and the purpose (meeting people's and organisations' information needs)."},{"criterion":"(b) Usages with benefits","marks":4,"look":["book|reserv","check.?in|kiosk","pay|card|bill","because|so that|which means|benefit|saves|increas|reduc"],"min":3,"strong":"Two usages with clear business benefits.","weak":"For each usage, add the benefit: \"Online booking lets overseas guests reserve rooms at any time, so the hotel gains sales even when the desk is closed.\""},{"criterion":"(c) Limitations and how to reduce them","marks":4,"look":["power|electric|outage","garbage|gigo|input|cost|expens|obsole|judg|skill|train|secur","generator|ups|backup|battery|inverter","validat|check|train|encrypt|password|budget|support contract|upgrade plan"],"min":3,"strong":"Two limitations, each with a realistic way to reduce it.","weak":"Pair each limitation with a remedy: power cut and a generator or UPS; GIGO and validation checks; skills gap and staff training."},{"criterion":"(d) Comparing IEEE and BCS with a recommendation","marks":5,"look":["ieee|institute of electrical","bcs|chartered institute|british computer","standard|802","chartered|citp|accredit|certif|code of conduct","whereas|however|while|on the other hand","recommend|therefore|on balance|more useful"],"min":4,"strong":"Matched comparison and a recommendation justified by her role.","weak":"Compare focus and activities (\"IEEE develops standards such as 802.11, whereas BCS awards Chartered IT Professional status\"), then recommend one and justify it with the officer's work."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Information Technology is the use of computers, software, networks and telecommunications to capture, process, store, transmit and present data and information. Its purpose is to meet the information needs of people and organisations; for the Bayview Hotel, that means handling bookings, guests and payments efficiently.</p><p><b>(b)</b> First, IT is used for online reservations: guests book rooms over the Internet from anywhere at any time. This benefits the hotel because it can take bookings from overseas visitors even when the front desk is closed, increasing occupancy. Second, IT is used for check-in kiosks and card billing: charges are calculated automatically and paid by card. This speeds up check-in and check-out and reduces cash-handling errors, so staff can spend more time on guest service.</p><p><b>(c)</b> One limitation is dependence on power. At the Bayview Hotel a power cut stopped the system for three hours, so guests could not be checked in and the hotel's reputation suffered. This could be reduced by installing a UPS and a backup generator for the front-desk computers and network equipment. A second limitation is \"garbage in, garbage out\": if a receptionist keys the wrong departure date, the system bills the guest incorrectly because it cannot judge whether the input is true. This could be reduced by validation checks (for example, a departure date must be after the arrival date) and by training staff to confirm details with the guest.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE) focuses on electrical, electronic and computing engineering; it develops technical standards such as 802.11 for Wi-Fi and publishes research. BCS, The Chartered Institute for IT, focuses on the IT profession and practice; it awards Chartered IT Professional (CITP) status, accredits degrees and publishes a Code of Conduct. For the hotel's IT officer, whose job is running the reservation system, the network and user support, I recommend BCS, because CITP status and its practice-focused certifications would recognise and develop exactly the skills she uses every day. IEEE would be the better choice only if her role centred on designing or engineering network hardware.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Part (c) is the discriminator: award the remedy marks only when the remedy matches the limitation (a generator does nothing for GIGO). In (d), a list of each body's activities without comparison words caps at 3; full marks need a recommendation that is justified by the officer's role. Accept a well-justified recommendation of IEEE if the candidate argues that the hotel's Wi-Fi and network work is central to her job.</p>"}
