     "up": {"title":"Match the generation to its technology (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>This time the cards hide the switching component. Match each era to its memory, software and users.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Pre-electronic era","r":"Geared calculators and punched-card instructions, with no electronic switching at all"},{"l":"Electromechanical bridge (early 1940s)","r":"Relays and switches in the Z3 and Harvard Mark I: automatic, but slow and noisy"},{"l":"First generation","r":"Magnetic drums, punched-card input, binary machine language and users limited to governments and research"},{"l":"Second generation","r":"Magnetic core memory, tape storage, assembly language and the first widely sold business computers such as the IBM 1401"},{"l":"Third generation","r":"Keyboards and monitors, multiprogramming, time-sharing and cabinet-sized minicomputers"},{"l":"Fourth generation","r":"Semiconductor RAM, graphical user interfaces, application packages and computers in ordinary homes"},{"l":"Fifth generation (as often described)","r":"Goals such as machine reasoning, natural language and massively parallel processing"}],"win":"Well done. Each generation's component brought its own memory, software and users. Use these clues to support an outline answer."}
