     "up": {"title":"Mainframe or supercomputer? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A regional climate research centre in Barbados and a bank with branches across the Eastern Caribbean both say they need \"the most powerful computer we can get\". The bank processes millions of card transactions a day for thousands of users; the centre wants to run high-resolution hurricane and climate models. Which recommendation is BEST?","options":[{"t":"A supercomputer (or shared access to one) for the climate centre, because it needs maximum speed on single enormous simulations; a mainframe for the bank, because it needs reliable handling of huge transaction volumes for many simultaneous users.","fb":"Correct. You matched each category to its workload rather than to the word \"powerful\".","ok":true},{"t":"Networked microcomputers for both, because they are cheaper.","fb":"Ordinary PCs cannot meet either workload reliably: neither the bank's transaction volume nor the centre's modelling."},{"t":"A mainframe for both, because mainframes are the most reliable computers.","fb":"Reliability and transaction volume suit the bank, but the climate models need massive parallel calculating speed."},{"t":"A supercomputer for both, because the fastest computer is always the best choice.","fb":"Speed on one huge calculation is not the same as reliably handling millions of small transactions. A supercomputer would be costly and unsuited to the bank's workload."}]}
