     "up": {"title":"From plugboards to high-level languages (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The Computer Science Week talk has grown to ten slides, and the dates have been removed. Put them in order, earliest first.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Ada Lovelace publishes an algorithm for Babbage's Analytical Engine."},{"t":"ENIAC operators rewire plugboards and set switches to change the task."},{"t":"Programmers write binary machine language for one specific processor."},{"t":"Assembly language uses mnemonics such as LOAD and ADD, converted by an assembler."},{"t":"FORTRAN brings formula translation for scientists and engineers."},{"t":"COBOL is designed for business records such as payroll and accounts."},{"t":"BASIC is designed so that students can learn to program easily."},{"t":"C is created and used to rewrite the UNIX operating system."},{"t":"Python is released, emphasising readable code."},{"t":"Java and JavaScript appear, built for networked computers and the web."}],"win":"Excellent. FORTRAN (1957) before COBOL (1959), BASIC (1964) before C (1972), and Python (1991) before Java and JavaScript (1995)."}
