     "up": {"title":"What joined the networks together? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"A Lower Sixth student in Montego Bay writes in a mock exam: \"In 1983, the World Wide Web joined ARPANET and other networks into one Internet, and then DNS let people send email for the first time.\" How many errors does the statement contain, and what are they?","options":[{"t":"One error: it should say TCP/IP, not the World Wide Web.","fb":"That is one error, but there is a second: email had existed since 1971, long before DNS."},{"t":"No errors, because the Web and the Internet are the same thing.","fb":"The Internet is the network; the Web is a service that runs on it, created between 1989 and 1991."},{"t":"Two errors: TCP/IP, not the Web, joined the networks in 1983; and email had existed since 1971, so DNS did not make email possible (it let people use names instead of numeric addresses).","fb":"Correct. You caught both the wrong technology and the wrong cause-and-effect claim.","ok":true},{"t":"One error: the date should be 1969.","fb":"1969 is when ARPANET began. 1983 is the correct date for the switch to TCP/IP."}]}
