     "up": {"title":"Caribbean connections (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>You are now advising a guesthouse in Portsmouth, Dominica, after a damaging storm season. Choose the BEST answer each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"The guesthouse relies on one Internet provider that uses a single submarine cable. Which change would MOST improve its resilience?","options":["Buying faster laptops","Switching staff to 2G phones","Storing all bookings only in the cloud","Adding a second connection by a different route, such as satellite or a provider using another cable"],"answer":3,"fb":"Resilience comes from route diversity: a second, independent path keeps the business online if one link fails."},{"text":"Why do video calls over a geostationary satellite link feel delayed compared with fibre?","options":["Satellites have more bandwidth than fibre","Fibre cables use radio waves","Satellites only carry analogue signals","Signals travel tens of thousands of kilometres up and back down, adding latency"],"answer":3,"fb":"Distance adds delay (latency). Fibre also has far more bandwidth."},{"text":"A regulator such as ECTEL is set up after markets open to competition. What is its MAIN purpose?","options":["To lay all the submarine cables itself","To run the only mobile network","To oversee the opened telecommunications markets, for example licensing and fair competition","To build satellites"],"answer":2,"fb":"Regulators oversee competitive markets; they do not replace the operators."},{"text":"Why did liberalisation around 2000 spread mobile phones faster than new technology alone would have?","options":["It made SMS free forever","It banned landlines","It replaced fibre with telegraph cables","Competition lowered prices and operators raced to cover the islands"],"answer":3,"fb":"Competing operators cut prices and extended coverage, so ownership grew quickly."},{"text":"After a storm, mobile data is down but basic voice and SMS still work in parts of the island. Which service should the guesthouse use to send short updates to guests?","options":["A dial-up modem","SMS text messages","The telegraph","4G video streaming"],"answer":1,"fb":"SMS needs very little bandwidth and works on basic networks when mobile data fails."},{"text":"A student claims the Caribbean \"skipped\" dial-up and went straight to mobile broadband. Which response is MOST accurate?","options":["False: the Internet arrived with telegraph cables","True: satellites provided all access until 2010","False: the Internet reached most territories in the 1990s through dial-up from the national telephone companies, before broadband and mobile data","True: the Internet arrived in the region with 4G"],"answer":2,"fb":"Dial-up came first in the 1990s; broadband and mobile data followed in the 2000s."}],"win":"Well evaluated. Resilience needs route diversity, competition needs regulation, and low-bandwidth services such as SMS matter most in emergencies."}
