     "up": {"title":"Spot the errors in the timeline (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>This revision timeline looks convincing, but four lines contain errors in a date, a \"first\" or a technology.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY line that contains an error.","lines":["1. Colossus (1943 to 1944) was an electronic code-breaking machine built for one task.","2. The transistor was developed at Bell Labs in 1957.","3. The IBM System/360 (1964) was a family of compatible models.","4. The Intel 4004 (1971) was the first integrated circuit.","5. Tim Berners-Lee created the World Wide Web at CERN between 1989 and 1991.","6. ARPANET adopted TCP/IP on 1 January 1983.","7. Telstar (1962) was the first transatlantic fibre-optic cable.","8. 2G GSM introduced digital mobile calls and SMS.","9. The CDC 6600 (1964) is generally regarded as the first supercomputer.","10. MS-DOS (1981) gave the IBM PC a graphical user interface."],"correct":[1,3,6,9],"win":"Well spotted. The transistor came in 1947; the 4004 was the first commercial microprocessor (ICs date from 1958 to 1959); Telstar was a satellite (TAT-8 in 1988 was the first fibre cable); and MS-DOS used a command line.","miss":"Check each \"first\" and each technology carefully. Four lines are wrong: one date, one \"first\", one technology and one interface."}
