     "up": {"title":"What turned it into information? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"Leeward Hardware in St. John's, Antigua exports 12,000 till records to a spreadsheet. The manager calculates that plywood sales in the seven days before each hurricane warning averaged 3.4 times normal weekly sales. She later uses this figure to set next season's stock order. Which statement BEST explains why \"3.4 times normal sales\" is information while the 12,000 records are data?","options":[{"t":"The figure has been calculated from the records and compared with a baseline in a stated context, so it is meaningful to the manager.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Calculation and comparison with normal sales, linked to hurricane warnings, give the figure context and meaning."},{"t":"The figure is much shorter than the records, so it is easier to remember.","fb":"Summarising does reduce volume, but size is not the test. A short, meaningless number is still data."},{"t":"The figure was used to set next season's stock order, so it is information.","fb":"Using information to make a decision is knowledge. The figure was already information before she used it."},{"t":"The records are qualitative, whereas the figure is quantitative.","fb":"Both are quantitative: quantities and prices are counted. The type of data does not decide whether it is data or information."},{"t":"The figure was produced by a computer, whereas the records were keyed in by cashiers.","fb":"Who or what did the processing does not matter: people can process data too. The key point is the result has meaning."}]}
