     "up": {"title":"Match the trust characteristic (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>These situations come from across the region, and several look alike. Decide whether the meaning was <b>changed</b>, <b>hidden</b>, <b>contradicted</b>, <b>not understood</b>, <b>read differently</b> or <b>confirmed</b>.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Distortion","r":"A ministry press release says 1,500 homes were damaged; after it is retyped at a radio station, it is read on air as 15,000"},{"l":"Disguise","r":"A Kingston supermarket advert shows a price graph whose axis starts at $95, so a $2 rise looks enormous"},{"l":"Inconsistency","r":"The Barbados licensing office and the car's insurer hold two different chassis numbers for the same vehicle"},{"l":"Incomprehensible","r":"A Grenadian farmer receives a soil report written only in laboratory codes such as \"CEC 8 meq/100 g\", with no explanation"},{"l":"Subject to interpretation","r":"A Trinidad bank survey shows 48% of customers \"sometimes\" bank by phone; marketing calls it growth, while the risk team calls it weak uptake"},{"l":"Reliable","r":"Monthly rainfall totals from a calibrated Dominica weather station agree with two nearby gauges"}],"win":"Very good. Distortion changes the content, disguise hides or bends its meaning, and inconsistency shows that at least one copy is wrong. Those distinctions are exactly what Paper 01 tests."}
