     "up": {"title":"What makes information worth having? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>In each situation one \"worth\" characteristic is <b>missing or at stake</b>. Match carefully: value and relevance are close, and so are timeliness and lifespan.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Value","r":"A report on staff parking at a Montego Bay hotel is accurate and on time, but no decision changes because of it"},{"l":"Relevance","r":"A Trinidad bakery planning its Divali orders is sent last year's electricity tariff schedule"},{"l":"Timeliness","r":"An accurate storm advisory reaches a St. Kitts fishing co-operative after the boats have already sailed"},{"l":"Completeness","r":"A cargo manifest at Port Castries lists every container but no weights, so the crane operator cannot plan lifts"},{"l":"Lifespan","r":"A Georgetown exchange bureau is still quoting from last Friday's rate sheet on Tuesday"},{"l":"Information as a commodity","r":"A forecasting company's paid marine reports lose sales after a customer posts free copies online"}],"win":"Excellent. Relevance asks whether information is about this decision; value asks whether it improves the outcome. Timeliness asks whether it arrived in time; lifespan asks whether it is still current."}
