     "up": {"title":"Interviewing people: advantage or disadvantage? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Jamal, an Upper Six student in Roseau, Dominica, is interviewing people about how residents got news during Hurricane Maria in 2017. His diary includes advantages, disadvantages and the steps he took to <b>reduce</b> the disadvantages. Sort each note.</p>","main":"Is this an advantage, a disadvantage, or a way to reduce a disadvantage?","bins":["Advantage of people as a source","Disadvantage of people as a source","Way to reduce a disadvantage"],"items":[{"t":"A retired radio engineer explains how a ham radio operator in Portsmouth kept contact with Guadeloupe, something never published.","bin":0,"fb":"Unpublished, experiential knowledge that exists only in people: an advantage."},{"t":"Two neighbours give different dates for when the phone network came back.","bin":1,"fb":"Memory errors, and no record to settle the difference: a disadvantage."},{"t":"The hospital director cancels the interview twice before Jamal's deadline.","bin":1,"fb":"Availability: experts may be busy, and interviews take time to arrange."},{"t":"Jamal records each interview, with permission, and checks the dates against newspaper archives.","bin":2,"fb":"A recording and a documentary check reduce the memory and verification problems."},{"t":"Everyone Jamal has interviewed lives in Roseau, so he hears nothing about the east-coast villages.","bin":1,"fb":"Limited to a few people's experience, so not representative: a disadvantage."},{"t":"He asks the official for the department's logbook to support his claims.","bin":2,"fb":"Checking a claim against a document reduces the effect of bias."},{"t":"He adds interviews in four other parishes, including the east coast.","bin":2,"fb":"A wider sample reduces the problem of an unrepresentative view."},{"t":"A district nurse describes what happened in last week's storm drill, which no report has covered yet.","bin":0,"fb":"People are often more current than published sources: an advantage."},{"t":"When a farmer mentions \"the second wave\", Jamal asks a follow-up question and learns she means a flood two days later.","bin":0,"fb":"Interactive: he could clarify at once. An advantage no printed source offers."},{"t":"A council official stresses how quickly his department responded and skips the complaints.","bin":1,"fb":"A one-sided, self-serving account: bias, a disadvantage."}],"win":"Excellent. A Grade One discussion does not stop at the disadvantage: it shows how a researcher can reduce it, by recording, widening the sample and checking claims against documents."}
