     "up": {"title":"Print sources: make the right call (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A Lower Six class from Belize City is working in the school library. Each situation involves a trade-off between print sources. Choose the BEST judgement.</p>","items":[{"text":"Tariq must explain how the Internet developed, for an essay due in a month. The library's only book on it was published in 2009. What is the BEST approach?","options":["Use only the book, because edited books are always accurate","Use the book for the early history and check recent developments in a current journal or magazine","Avoid the book entirely because it is old"],"answer":1,"fb":"Age matters less for early history, which has not changed; recent developments need a more current source. Combining them uses each source's strength."},{"text":"The library catalogue says the library owns \"Caribbean Computing Today\", but the shelf is empty. What does this show about catalogues?","options":["A catalogue describes and locates items; it cannot hold their content, and the item may be on loan or misplaced","The book was never published","The catalogue is inaccurate and should not be used"],"answer":0,"fb":"A catalogue is a finding aid. It tells you an item exists and where it should be, not what it says."},{"text":"Shania finds two articles on cybercrime in Jamaica: one in a research journal and one in a popular technology magazine. Which comparison is BEST?","options":["The journal is more current; the magazine is more detailed","Both are equally reliable because both are periodicals","The journal is more detailed and evidence-based; the magazine is more readable and usually more current"],"answer":2,"fb":"Journals win on depth and expert checking; magazines win on readability and speed of publication."},{"text":"Mr. Hodge uses a printed supplier catalogue to choose printers for the library. Which caution matters MOST?","options":["Catalogues are traditional sources, so they cannot be trusted","Catalogues report original research, so they must be cited","Prices and models may be out of date, and the seller presents its products favourably, so he should check independent reviews"],"answer":2,"fb":"Two weaknesses of product catalogues: seller bias and printed details that date quickly."},{"text":"Devon wants expert evidence on solar power in small island states, but finds the journal articles very technical. What should he do?","options":["Read a magazine overview first, then use the journal articles' abstracts and conclusions for the evidence","Switch entirely to a magazine","Use a newspaper editorial instead"],"answer":0,"fb":"The magazine builds understanding; the journal supplies the expert evidence. Abandoning the journal would weaken his essay."},{"text":"A newspaper report written on the night of a road accident in Castries gives the wrong number of vehicles; the next day's edition corrects it. Which disadvantage does this show?","options":["Newspapers are never current","Writing to a deadline leads to errors that are corrected later","Newspapers need a device to read"],"answer":1,"fb":"Newspapers are current because they are written fast, and that speed is also why errors appear."},{"text":"Jaden's magazine review praises a gaming console on the page facing a full-page advert for the same console. What should he conclude?","options":["The review is as reliable as a journal article","Magazines never carry advertising","Advertising may have influenced the review, so he should compare it with other reviews"],"answer":2,"fb":"Magazines depend on advertisers, which can influence content. Comparing several reviews reduces the risk."}],"win":"Well judged. Each print source has a strength and a matching weakness, and the best researchers combine sources so one covers the other's gap."}
