     "up": {"title":"Match the source to its description (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each description gives a distinguishing feature, not just a definition. Match each source to the feature that identifies it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Journal","r":"Its articles are often checked by other experts before publication and end with reference lists"},{"l":"Magazine","r":"Readable reviews by staff writers, printed alongside paid advertising"},{"l":"Newspaper","r":"Written to a daily deadline, so errors may be corrected in the next issue"},{"l":"Catalogue","r":"Tells you an item exists and where it is, but does not contain its content"},{"l":"Library","r":"Gives free access to many other sources, including paid databases, through trained staff"},{"l":"Book","r":"Treats one subject at length with chapters and an index, but takes months or years to produce"},{"l":"CD-ROM","r":"About 700 MB, read-only, and usable without the Internet"},{"l":"DVD","r":"About 4.7 GB on one layer, read with a shorter-wavelength laser"}],"win":"Excellent. Knowing the feature that sets each source apart is what turns a definition into a precise \"describe\" or \"distinguish\" answer."}
