     "up": {"title":"Database or web site? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Leah, a Flamboyant Heights College student, is writing about renewable energy policy in small island states. The library's electronic database returns 40 peer-reviewed articles from 2019 to 2025. A web search returns two million results, including her government's energy ministry page, updated yesterday with new solar targets. Which plan is BEST?","options":[{"t":"Use the database for peer-reviewed research and the ministry's official page for the latest targets, checking the date and author of each.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Each source covers the other's weakness: the database gives checked research, and the official page gives current policy that research has not yet caught up with."},{"t":"Use the database because, unlike the web, it can be searched without an Internet connection.","fb":"Most electronic databases are reached online, so Leah needs a connection for both."},{"t":"Use only the web search, because two million results give better coverage.","fb":"More results mean information overload and variable quality, not better coverage of reliable research."},{"t":"Use only the database, because web sites are never reliable.","fb":"Too absolute. An official ministry page is a reliable and more current source for the latest policy figures."}]}
