     "up": {"title":"Order the sources after an event (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>An oil slick is spotted off the coast of Tobago. Put the sources in the order in which information would TYPICALLY become available, earliest first, including the step at which researchers can search for it.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"A fisherman radios the coast guard to describe the slick he can see."},{"t":"Fishermen post photos and videos of the slick on social media."},{"t":"A news web site publishes a short report quoting the coast guard."},{"t":"The next printed newspaper carries a fuller report with a map and interviews."},{"t":"A monthly magazine runs a feature on oil-spill risks in Caribbean waters."},{"t":"A marine science journal publishes research measuring the damage to nearby reefs."},{"t":"The journal article is indexed in an electronic database, where students can search for it."},{"t":"A book on Caribbean environmental disasters includes a chapter on the spill."}],"win":"Exactly right. Information moves from people and social media to web sites and newspapers, then to magazines, journals, databases and books: deeper and more checked at each step, but less current."}
