     "up": {"title":"Researching for the Turks and Caicos tourism project (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Amara's project has been selected for a regional student research competition. Her question is now harder: \"How did cruise tourism in the Turks and Caicos Islands recover after Hurricane Irma, and what does the evidence show about the reliability of different sources?\" She has three weeks.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Where should Amara start?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Begin by interviewing people at the cruise pier before reading anything.","to":"ok1","fb":"Primary evidence matters, but without background she will not know which questions to ask."},{"t":"Copy the main facts from the wiki article into her report.","to":"bad1","fb":"A wiki is a starting point. Anyone can edit it, so copying it unchecked is risky."},{"t":"Read a recent review article from the library database and a wiki article for an overview, checking dates and noting their references.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Secondary sources give a quick overview, and their references lead to primary evidence."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>For the same year, a news web site, the port's official statistics and a viral social media post give three different cruise passenger totals. What does she do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Average the three figures to be fair to every source.","to":"bad2","fb":"Averaging an official figure with a rumour produces a number nobody published. Weigh sources; do not blend them."},{"t":"Use the largest figure because it shows the strongest recovery.","to":"bad2","fb":"Choosing the figure that suits the argument is bias, not research."},{"t":"Use the official statistics as the main figure, note that the news site rounded it, and explain why the viral post is unreliable.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. She weighed each source's reliability and turned the conflict into evidence for her second question."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>She now needs first-hand evidence from people. Which plan is best?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Interview only her uncle, who runs a tour company.","to":"ok2","fb":"Convenient and first-hand, but one person with an interest in the result gives a narrow, possibly biased view."},{"t":"Interview a tour operator, a Leeward Highway shop owner and a taxi driver, recording with permission and checking their claims against the figures.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Several perspectives reduce bias, and checking against the data reduces memory errors."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>How should she present her sources in the final report?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Cite every source in APA style and add a short evaluation of each source's strengths and limits.","to":"good","fb":"This answers the second half of her question and shows Grade One source evaluation."},{"t":"List the sources at the end with no evaluation.","to":"ok3","fb":"The citations are there, but her question asked about reliability, which a plain list does not address."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Competition standard.</b> Amara combined secondary overview, official figures and recorded primary interviews, resolved conflicting data by judging reliability, and evaluated every source. The judges single out her treatment of the conflicting passenger totals.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Slow start.</b> Her first interviews wander because she does not yet know the key events and figures. Try again and build an overview first.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Narrow evidence.</b> The judges note that her only interviewee has a business interest in a positive story. Try again with several perspectives.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Half the question answered.</b> The research is sound, but she never evaluates the reliability of her sources, which was part of her question. Try again.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Problem.</b> Two copied \"facts\" had been changed by an anonymous editor and were wrong. Try again and use the wiki only as a starting point.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Problem.</b> Her figure matches no official source, and the judges question every other number in the report. Try again and weigh the reliability of each source.</p>","end":"bad"}}}
