     "up": {"title":"Weighing a disadvantage (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A teacher in Dominica must choose one encyclopaedia for her classroom: printed, on CD-ROM, or online. The classroom laptops have no optical drives, and the Internet often fails during the rainy season. Which argument is MOST valid?","options":[{"t":"The CD-ROM is best because it works without the Internet.","fb":"Working offline is a real advantage, but it is irrelevant when the laptops cannot read the disc."},{"t":"The printed encyclopaedia is the best choice here: the CD-ROM cannot be read on these laptops and the online version fails during outages, even though print is the slowest to search and the hardest to keep current.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. This weighs the valid disadvantage of each alternative in this situation and admits print's own weakness."},{"t":"The online version is best because it is always up to date.","fb":"Currency is a real advantage, but outages are frequent, so the source would fail when it is needed."},{"t":"The CD-ROM is worst because students could edit its content.","fb":"A pressed CD-ROM is read-only. That disadvantage belongs to a wiki."}]}
