     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> A <b>primary source</b> is original, first-hand evidence created by someone directly involved in an event, at or near the time. Ravi's recorded interview with the electricity company line worker is primary because the worker personally restored power in Philipsburg after the storm. A <b>secondary source</b> describes, summarises or interprets primary sources and is produced later by someone not directly involved. The 2022 magazine article is secondary because the writer reviews the recovery using other people's accounts and data.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One <b>advantage</b> of primary sources is that they give specific, first-hand detail that may not be published anywhere else. The line worker can describe exactly how crews worked and how residents got news without power, which no textbook covers, so Ravi's research becomes specific to Sint Maarten. One <b>disadvantage</b> is that primary sources can be biased or affected by memory. The worker is recalling events from 2017, may have forgotten details and may present his own team favourably, and his aunt's photographs show only Great Bay. Ravi should therefore check these accounts against secondary sources, such as the textbook, before drawing conclusions.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "<p>For \"distinguish\", reward the <b>contrast</b>: both definitions plus a justified example of each. In part (b), the advantage and disadvantage must be <b>applied to Ravi</b>; generic textbook statements earn one mark, not two.</p>",
