     "up": {"title":"Primary and secondary sources in a research task (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: primary and secondary sources, Grade One extension (15 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Classify</b> EACH of Ravi's four items as a primary or a secondary source, <b>justifying</b> each classification. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Discuss</b> ONE advantage and ONE disadvantage of primary sources, AND ONE advantage and ONE disadvantage of secondary sources, for Ravi's research. <i>(8 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommend</b> how Ravi should combine his sources to reach a reliable conclusion, explaining how each type makes up for a weakness of the other. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":250,"max":480,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> (i) The interview is ... because ... (ii) The textbook chapter is ... because ... (iii) The photographs are ... because ... (iv) The 2022 magazine article is ... because ...<br><b>(b)</b> One advantage of primary sources for Ravi is ... For example, ... One disadvantage is ... because ... One advantage of secondary sources is ... For example, ... One disadvantage is ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> Ravi should first ... then ... Where the sources disagree, he should ... This works because the primary sources ..., while the secondary sources ...</p>","checklist":["I classified all four items and gave a reason for each, based on closeness to the original.","I discussed an advantage and a disadvantage of primary sources, each applied to Ravi.","I discussed an advantage and a disadvantage of secondary sources, each applied to Ravi.","Every point names one of Ravi's actual items.","My recommendation explains how each type covers the other's weakness."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"(a) Four items classified and justified","marks":4,"look":["interview.{0,80}primary|primary.{0,80}interview","textbook.{0,80}secondary|secondary.{0,80}textbook","photo.{0,80}primary|primary.{0,80}photo","magazine.{0,80}secondary|secondary.{0,80}magazine"],"min":3,"strong":"All four items are classified correctly with reasons.","weak":"Interview and photographs: primary, because they were created by people directly involved at the time. Textbook and 2022 magazine article: secondary, because they summarise and interpret other sources later."},{"criterion":"(b) Primary sources: advantage and disadvantage in context","marks":4,"look":["primary","specific|first.?hand|unique|not.{0,20}(written|published)|detail|original","memory|bias|narrow|only (show|one)|great bay|forg|time|verify","line worker|interview|photo|aunt"],"min":3,"strong":"Both sides are explained using Ravi's primary items.","weak":"Advantage: the line worker gives first-hand detail found nowhere else. Disadvantage: his memories of 2017 may have faded, and the photographs show only Great Bay."},{"criterion":"(b) Secondary sources: advantage and disadvantage in context","marks":4,"look":["secondary","overview|quick|context|summar|compar|whole island|cheap|reference","interpret|bias|out of date|general|not specific|copied|error|opinion","textbook|magazine|2022"],"min":3,"strong":"Both sides are explained using Ravi's secondary items.","weak":"Advantage: the 2022 article gives an island-wide overview of the recovery. Disadvantage: the textbook is general to the Caribbean and the article includes the writer's interpretation."},{"criterion":"(c) Recommendation for combining sources","marks":3,"look":["first|start|begin|then|combin|both","check|compare|cross|confirm|verify|disagree","because|so that|cover|make up|weakness|strength"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear sequence that shows each type covering the other's weakness.","weak":"Recommend a sequence (overview from secondary sources, detail from primary ones, then cross-checking), and say which weakness each type covers."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> (i) The recorded interview with the electricity company line worker is <b>primary</b>, because he personally restored power in Philipsburg and describes his own experience. (ii) The textbook chapter on disaster management is <b>secondary</b>, because its author summarises and interprets other people's research on the whole Caribbean. (iii) His aunt's photographs of Great Bay are <b>primary</b>, because they are original images taken the morning after the storm. (iv) The 2022 magazine article is <b>secondary</b>, because it reviews the recovery five years later using other people's accounts and data.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One <b>advantage of primary sources</b> is that they give specific, first-hand detail that is not published anywhere else. The line worker can explain how crews prioritised hospitals and how residents got news while the power was off, so Ravi's findings are specific to Sint Maarten. One <b>disadvantage</b> is that primary sources can be narrow or affected by memory and bias. The worker is recalling events from 2017 and may present his own team favourably, and the photographs show only Great Bay, so on their own they cannot describe the whole island.</p><p>One <b>advantage of secondary sources</b> is that they give an overview and context that someone has already gathered. The 2022 magazine article draws together the recovery across the island, which Ravi could not collect himself in the time available. One <b>disadvantage</b> is that secondary sources contain the writer's interpretation and may not answer Ravi's specific question: the textbook is about disaster management across the Caribbean in general and says little about how Sint Maarten residents used information sources.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Ravi should start with the textbook and magazine article to understand the overall recovery and identify key events, then use the interview and photographs for specific, first-hand evidence about Philipsburg and Great Bay. Where the two disagree, for example over when power returned, he should check a third source such as newspaper archives. This works because the secondary sources supply the breadth that his primary items lack, while the primary sources supply the local, first-hand detail that the secondary sources miss.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>In (a), one mark per item, and only with a reason based on closeness to the original (not on medium). In (b), each of the four points needs an explanation applied to one of Ravi's items; generic textbook statements earn half marks at most. In (c), reward a sequence with cross-checking and an explicit statement of which weakness each type covers.</p>"}
