     "up": {"title":"Read the web page clues (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A CAPE IT student is selecting web sources for a report on coral reefs around Saba. These clues are harder: some look reassuring but prove little. Choose the BEST interpretation of each.</p>","items":[{"text":"www.uwi-research.edu/~kpeters/notes.html is a page in a lecturer's personal folder on a university server. How should it be treated?","options":["As a personal page: the university hosts it, but it may not have been approved","As an official, approved university publication","As a commercial page"],"answer":0,"fb":"A ~name folder usually holds a personal page. Hosting is not the same as organisational approval."},{"text":"www.reefsaver.org says it is a charity, but every page sells \"reef-safe\" sunscreen. What does the .org address tell her?","options":["It proves the site is a non-profit with no bias","It shows the site is academic","Anyone can register a .org name, so she must check who runs the site and why"],"answer":2,"fb":"A domain ending is a clue, not proof. The selling suggests a commercial purpose and possible bias."},{"text":"The page www.saba-marinepark.sx-info.com looks like the marine park's official site. What should she check first?","options":["Nothing: the park's logo proves it is official","The registered domain: it is sx-info.com, not the park's own name, so the page may be a look-alike","Whether the colours match the park's brochure"],"answer":1,"fb":"Logos are easy to copy. The part just before .com is the registered domain, and here it is not the park's."},{"text":"A page shows \"Last updated: 2 February 2026\", a named author with qualifications and a Webmaster contact, but it links only to its own shop pages. Which judgement is BEST?","options":["Currency and authorship are good signs, but linking only to its own shop suggests a commercial purpose, so test it for bias","Select it without further checks","Reject it because it has a shop"],"answer":0,"fb":"Weigh the clues together. Good signs do not cancel a possible bias; they mean the page deserves a closer check."},{"text":"Which clue BEST shows that authorities trust a page?","options":["It appears first in search results","It shows a copyright sign","It has thousands of likes","It is linked from the national library's research guide and a university course page"],"answer":3,"fb":"Links FROM reliable organisations show that they rely on the page. Rankings and likes measure popularity."},{"text":"A page's footer shows \"© 2026\", but the dive-permit fees on it are labelled \"2019 rates\". What should she conclude?","options":["Footer years often update automatically; the content is from 2019, so its currency for this year's fees is doubtful","The page is current because of the 2026 copyright","The page has been refereed"],"answer":0,"fb":"Check the date on the information itself, not just the footer."}],"win":"Well read. URLs, approval notes, dates and links are clues to be weighed together, not proofs to be accepted one at a time."}
