     "up": {"title":"Selecting sources for an outage report (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>You are the student intern preparing Cable Beach Residents' Association's report on electricity outages for a public meeting. The electricity company will send a representative, so every figure will be challenged. Apply the selection criteria carefully.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>A viral post claims \"The electricity company has had 40 outages this month and is hiding it\". Residents have also posted dated photos of dark streets. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Treat the viral figure as an unverified claim, but save residents' dated posts as possible first-hand evidence to check.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. You separated an unchecked claim from primary evidence that can be tested."},{"t":"Ignore everything on social media, including residents' own dated posts.","to":"ok1","fb":"Caution is right, but rejecting all social media throws away first-hand evidence that could be checked."},{"t":"Use \"40 outages\" as the report's headline because so many people shared it.","to":"bad1","fb":"Sharing is not evidence. The claim fails accuracy until it is verified."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>You have the electricity company's outage notice page with dated entries, the residents' own log of outage times and a 2014 overseas magazine article on power grids in the US Midwest. Which do you rely on?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The electricity company's dated notices and the residents' log, cross-checked against each other.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Both are current and local, and cross-checking tests their accuracy and completeness."},{"t":"Only the electricity company's notices, because the utility is the official source.","to":"ok2","fb":"Official and dated, but the electricity company has an interest in how outages are counted, so relying on it alone risks incompleteness."},{"t":"The magazine article, because it explains grids in depth.","to":"bad2","fb":"Deep, but it fails currency and cultural context: an old article about another country's grid and climate."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The sources disagree: The electricity company lists 9 outages, while residents logged 14, including 5 that lasted under five minutes. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use only the residents' 14, because residents are the ones affected.","to":"ok3","fb":"The residents' log is valuable, but choosing the side you sympathise with is bias in the other direction."},{"t":"Report both figures, explain that the electricity company may record only outages above a set length, and ask the electricity company for its definition before the meeting.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. You treated the difference as a completeness question and sought the information needed to resolve it."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>A board member hands you a leaflet from the PowerSafe Generators Ltd claiming outages will double next year. How do you treat it?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Include the prediction as a fact: the company knows about power.","to":"bad3","fb":"The company sells generators, so it gains if people fear outages."},{"t":"Note the company's commercial interest, treat the prediction as biased, and look for an independent source such as the regulator's published supply plan.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You identified bias and balanced it with independent evidence."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>A credible report.</b> You separated rumour from evidence, used current local sources, resolved a conflict by asking how outages are defined, and flagged the biased leaflet. The electricity company representative accepts your figures as fair. A Grade One answer would name every criterion you applied: accuracy, currency, cultural context, completeness and bias.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Over-cautious.</b> Your report has no first-hand evidence from residents, and members feel unheard. Try again and keep evidence that can be checked.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>One-sided.</b> Residents point out short outages that the electricity company's list leaves out, and the report looks incomplete. Try again and cross-check sources.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Challenged at the meeting.</b> The electricity company shows that five of the outages lasted seconds, and your report looks slanted. Try again and present both figures fairly.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Credibility lost.</b> The electricity company shows the real figure is far lower, and residents stop trusting the report. Try again and test the claim for accuracy.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Off target.</b> The report discusses Midwest snowstorms instead of Cable Beach. Try again and apply currency and cultural context.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Persuaded by a sales pitch.</b> The report repeats a generator advert as evidence. Try again and apply the bias criterion.</p>","end":"bad"}}}
