     "up": {"title":"Internet information as a criterion (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Tariq finds a well-designed web page on Caribbean scholarships. It appears first in his search results, has a .org address, has been shared 5,000 times and gives a deadline of \"30 June\" with no year. It also asks applicants to pay a US$25 \"processing fee\" on the page. Which evaluation BEST applies the criterion of characteristics of information on the Internet?","options":[{"t":"He should reject it because all Internet information is unreliable.","fb":"Too absolute. Many official and refereed sources are online; the criterion asks you to check, not to reject everything."},{"t":"It is accurate because 5,000 people have shared it.","fb":"Popularity spreads information; it does not verify it."},{"t":"Because anyone can publish, rank, copy and imitate content online, he should confirm the deadline and any fee on the scholarship provider's own site, and check the page's owner, date and purpose before relying on it.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The characteristics of online information explain each warning sign, and the response is to trace the information to its original, accountable source."},{"t":"The top search ranking and .org address show the page can be trusted.","fb":"Rankings can be bought or gamed, and anyone can register a .org name."}]}
