     "up": {"title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Pointe Bakery in Gros Islet, St. Lucia runs its orders by hand. The owner is not ready to automate, so she has also taken steps to <b>reduce</b> the problems of her manual system. Sort each note.</p>","main":"Strength, problem, or a way to reduce a problem?","bins":["Strength of manual processing","Problem with manual processing","Way to reduce a problem"],"items":[{"t":"The order book is kept in a waterproof box away from the leaking roof.","bin":2,"fb":"This protects the paper that holds the data, reducing the risk of loss."},{"t":"The owner records an unusual order for a three-tier guava cake in her own way.","bin":0,"fb":"Human creativity and flexibility with odd cases: a strength."},{"t":"During a three-day power cut, the bakery keeps taking orders in the notebook.","bin":0,"fb":"Manual processing does not depend on electricity or networks: a strength."},{"t":"A new assistant learns the order book in one morning.","bin":0,"fb":"Simple methods that need little training: a strength."},{"t":"Each regular customer's address is rewritten on every order sheet.","bin":1,"fb":"Reusing data is repetitive: a problem."},{"t":"Totals added by a tired assistant at closing time often contain mistakes.","bin":1,"fb":"Errors occur in manual output: a problem."},{"t":"Getting the week's orders ready to total takes all of Monday morning.","bin":1,"fb":"Slow to get data ready for processing: a problem."},{"t":"A second person re-adds each day's totals and initials the page.","bin":2,"fb":"A manual verification step reduces arithmetic errors."},{"t":"Finding every coconut-tart order from last month means reading every page.","bin":1,"fb":"Querying is difficult by hand: a problem."},{"t":"Regular customers' details are written once on index cards filed by surname.","bin":2,"fb":"Writing details once and looking them up reduces repetitive rewriting."}],"win":"Excellent. A Grade One discussion of manual processing weighs its strengths against its problems and shows how sensible procedures can reduce those problems, even without automation."}
