     "up": {"title":"Which approach has the edge? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Each situation turns on one factor: volume, speed, cost, flexibility, sharing, training or dependence on power. Decide which approach has the edge, and name the factor before you check the feedback.</p>","main":"Which approach suits this situation better?","bins":["Manual processing","Automated processing"],"items":[{"t":"A village council needs a records method volunteers can use with no training.","bin":0,"fb":"Training: manual methods are simple to learn."},{"t":"A credit union needs every branch to see the same balance at the same moment.","bin":1,"fb":"Sharing: many users in many places can use one record at once."},{"t":"A church secretary must handle a donation form with an unusual note asking to split the gift.","bin":0,"fb":"Flexibility: human judgement handles cases a program was not set up for."},{"t":"An exam board must mark 30,000 multiple-choice answer sheets in a week.","bin":1,"fb":"Volume and accuracy: OMR marks consistently at speed."},{"t":"A records office needs a back-up method that still works when its server is down.","bin":0,"fb":"Dependence: a paper fallback keeps essential records usable."},{"t":"A clinic wants the lowest cost per appointment once its system is running.","bin":1,"fb":"Running cost: low cost per transaction once set up."},{"t":"Three days after a hurricane, a shelter in Barbuda must register arrivals with no power.","bin":0,"fb":"Dependence: manual processing works without electricity."},{"t":"A supermarket must find, in seconds, every product with fewer than 20 items in stock.","bin":1,"fb":"Querying: a database finds records by any condition instantly."},{"t":"A fruit stand making ten sales a day wants the lowest possible start-up cost.","bin":0,"fb":"Start-up cost: a notebook and cash box cost almost nothing."},{"t":"A telecoms company must produce 40,000 accurate bills overnight.","bin":1,"fb":"Volume and speed: automation handles huge volumes quickly."}],"win":"Well judged. Manual wins on start-up cost, training, flexibility and independence from power; automated wins on volume, speed, sharing, querying and cost per transaction. Strong systems often use automation with a manual back-up."}
