     "up": {"title":"Manual system, automated equivalent (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each automated system replaced a manual one. Match the automated system to the specific manual problem it removed.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Online library catalogue","r":"Finding books by subject meant checking every card, because cards were filed by author"},{"l":"Electronic class register","r":"Counting each student's absences meant re-reading every page of the register"},{"l":"Point-of-sale system","r":"Stock levels were known only after a monthly count by hand"},{"l":"Mobile banking app","r":"Customers saw their balance only when a teller updated the passbook"},{"l":"Online reservation system","r":"Two agents could sell the same seat because each kept a separate paper chart"},{"l":"Electronic health record","r":"Only one doctor at a time, in one building, could use a patient's folder"},{"l":"Optical ballot scanner","r":"Counting thousands of ballots by hand took all night and often needed recounts"}],"win":"Correct. Automation changed specific things: retrieval by any field, automatic calculation, real-time updates, one shared record and many simultaneous users. Naming the specific change earns more than \"it is faster\"."}
