     "up": {"title":"Which requests are slow by hand? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The primary school keeps 900 student folders in metal cabinets, filed alphabetically by surname. Some requests use the filing key in ways that are still quick; others cut across it.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY request that would be SLOW to satisfy using this manual file.","lines":["1. Find the folder for a student named Richardson.","2. List every student who lives in Bolans.","3. Pull out every folder with a surname beginning with M.","4. Find the student whose ID number is 2041.","5. Re-arrange all 900 folders by date of birth.","6. Find all students with the surname Hodge, to contact the family.","7. Count how many students chose IT as their favourite subject.","8. Return the Arrindell folder to its place.","9. List all students born in 2010 who live in Jolly Harbour.","10. Add a folder for a new student called Brooks."],"correct":[1,3,4,6,8],"win":"Correct. Requests 2, 4, 5, 7 and 9 use fields that are not the filing key (district, ID number, date of birth, subject), so every folder must be handled. Surnames beginning with M, or all the Hodges, sit together in the file, so those are quick.","miss":"Look again. Anything that uses the surname, including a group of surnames that sit together, is quick. Any other field, such as an ID number or a combination of district and year, means checking all 900 folders."}
