     "up": {"title":"Defining feedback (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"At the Harbour View Supermarket in Georgetown, each sale reduces the stock count. When the count for bottled water drops below 20 cases, the system adds bottled water to the next purchase order without anyone touching it. Which statement BEST explains why this is feedback?","options":[{"t":"Because a result of processing (the stock level) is compared with a re-order level, and the outcome automatically becomes new input (a purchase order), with no person involved.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. This is an automated feedback loop: output is evaluated against a rule and looped straight back into the system as input."},{"t":"Because the purchase order is printed, which makes it output.","fb":"Printing the order is output, but that alone is not feedback. The key is that a result is evaluated and fed back in as new input."},{"t":"Because the stock file is saved after every sale.","fb":"Saving the stock file is storage. Feedback needs an evaluation that sends something back to input."},{"t":"Because the supplier later delivers the water to the store.","fb":"The delivery is a physical result outside the information system. The feedback is the automatic re-order triggered by the stock level."}]}
