     "model": "<p><b>Input.</b> Booking data is collected when a customer completes the online booking form or tells the front-desk clerk their name, dive date, trip and number of divers. The clerk enters walk-in bookings into the booking system, so all bookings are captured in one place.</p>\n<p><b>Processing.</b> At the end of the week the bookings are sorted by trip and date, the number of divers on each trip is counted, and the income is calculated as divers × price per trip and totalled. The owner's software also analyses which trips were full and which had spare places.</p>\n<p><b>Output.</b> The results are presented as a weekly report with a table and a bar chart of divers per trip, and disseminated to the owner by email.</p>\n<p><b>Storage.</b> Each booking and each weekly report is stored in the booking database and backed up, so they can be retrieved later, for example to compare this July with last July.</p>\n<p><b>Feedback.</b> This stage is optional. The owner evaluates the report and sees that the wreck dive is always over-booked while the reef dive is half-empty. She decides to add a waiting-list option to the booking form. This update loops back to the input stage, so next week's data is more useful.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "<p>Award 1 mark per stage for a correct explanation and 1 for an applied dive-centre activity, grouping output and storage as shown. The feedback marks require the idea of a <b>loop back to input</b>; \"the owner looks at the report\" alone earns only 1. Candidates who merely list the five stage names should score no more than 2.</p>",
