     "up": {"title":"Manual versus automated: the school library (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Paper 02 extended question: manual versus automated information processing, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Describe</b> how data is stored and retrieved in the library's current manual system. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> how the input, processing and output of a book loan would differ if the library used the automated system. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Describe</b> how storing, retrieving and transmitting would work in the automated system, referring to how, where, what and when. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> whether the school should automate the library. Give TWO advantages and TWO disadvantages, suggest ONE way to reduce a disadvantage, and reach a justified recommendation. <i>(6 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":380,"max":650,"frame":"<p><b>(a) Storage:</b> Catalogue cards are ... Loan cards are ... <b>Retrieval:</b> By author ..., but by subject or title ...<br><b>(b) Input:</b> Manually ..., whereas automated ... <b>Processing:</b> Manually ..., whereas ... <b>Output:</b> Manually ..., whereas ...<br><b>(c) How:</b> ... <b>Where:</b> ... <b>What:</b> ... <b>When:</b> ... Records are retrieved by ... and transmitted by ...<br><b>(d)</b> Advantages: ... and ... Disadvantages: ... and ... This could be reduced by ... On balance, I recommend ... because ...</p>","checklist":["In (a) I described storage medium and order, and why retrieval is quick only by the filing key.","In (b) I compared input, processing and output with \"whereas\", using syllabus terms.","In (c) I answered how, where, what and when, and covered retrieving and transmitting.","In (d) I gave two advantages and two disadvantages, each with a reason.","I suggested a practical way to reduce one disadvantage.","My recommendation is justified by the library's size and use."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"(a) Manual storage and retrieval","marks":4,"look":["card|drawer|box|cabinet","alphabetic|author|due date|order","retriev|find|search|look","slow|every card|subject|title|key"],"min":3,"strong":"Storage is described precisely and retrieval is linked to the filing key.","weak":"Cards filed by author in drawers; loan cards by due date in a box. Retrieval is quick by author but needs every card checked for a subject or title."},{"criterion":"(b) Input, processing and output compared","marks":6,"look":["barcode|scan","hand|writ|loan card","due date|overdue|fine|calculat|sort","automatic|software|system|instant","email|sms|screen|report","whereas|however|while|instead"],"min":5,"strong":"All three stages are contrasted, each with both sides.","weak":"For each stage give the manual method, then \"whereas\" and the automated method: handwritten loan card versus scanning barcodes; sorting cards by hand versus calculating due dates automatically; handwritten slips versus email reminders."},{"criterion":"(c) Automated storing, retrieving and transmitting","marks":4,"look":["database|server|disk|drive|cloud","record|loan|catalogue|backup|history","immediately|instant|each loan|nightly|daily|schedul","search|query|any field|transmit|email|network|send"],"min":3,"strong":"How, where, what and when are all addressed, with retrieval and transmission.","weak":"Say records are saved as database records (how) on a school server with a cloud backup (where), including catalogue, loan and borrower data (what), at each loan plus nightly backups (when); then show retrieval by any field and transmission of reminders over the network."},{"criterion":"(d) Evaluation and justified recommendation","marks":6,"look":["advantage|benefit","search|any field|faster|accura|reminder|several users","disadvantage|drawback","cost|power|train|outage|depend|label","reduce|generator|ups|paper back.?up|training|phase","recommend|on balance|therefore|should"],"min":5,"strong":"Two advantages and two disadvantages, a mitigation and a recommendation justified by the library's situation.","weak":"Give two developed points each way, one mitigation such as a paper loan book or UPS, and a recommendation that refers to 6,000 books and hundreds of users."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> In the manual system, data is stored on paper. Each book has a catalogue card, filed alphabetically by author's surname in drawers, and loans are recorded on handwritten loan cards kept in a box in order of due date. To retrieve a book's details, the librarian goes to the drawer for the author's initial, which is quick because author is the filing key. However, retrieving all the books on a subject, or a book by title, means checking every one of the 6,000 cards, and finding everything one student has borrowed means checking every loan card.</p><p><b>(b) Input.</b> Manually, the librarian writes the student's name, the title and the due date on a loan card, which is slow and may contain errors, whereas in the automated system the book's barcode and the student's ID card are scanned, so the data is captured in seconds and exactly. <b>Processing.</b> Manually, the librarian works out the due date, sorts the card into the box and checks the box by hand for overdue books, whereas the automated system calculates the due date, records the loan and identifies overdue loans instantly. <b>Output.</b> Manually, reminder slips are handwritten and delivered to form rooms, whereas the automated system disseminates email reminders automatically and can display or print an overdue report at any time.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>How:</b> each book, borrower and loan is saved as a record in a database, indexed so it can be searched. <b>Where:</b> on the school's server, with a backup copy in cloud storage away from the building. <b>What:</b> catalogue records, borrower records, current loans, loan history and logs of changes. <b>When:</b> each loan and return is stored immediately as it happens, with an automatic backup every night. Records are <b>retrieved</b> by a search on any field, such as title, subject or student, and several users can search at once from different computers. Reminders and reports are <b>transmitted</b> over the school network by email.</p><p><b>(d)</b> One advantage is flexible retrieval: students can search by title, author or subject from any school computer, so books are found and used more. A second advantage is accuracy and time saved at the desk: scanning removes handwriting errors and issues a book in seconds during busy break times. One disadvantage is the set-up cost of computers, software, scanners and labelling 6,000 books, plus training. A second disadvantage is dependence on power: in an outage, loans cannot be recorded. This could be reduced by keeping a paper loan book for outages and entering those loans when power returns, or by fitting a UPS to the desk computer. On balance, I recommend automating, because a library of 6,000 books used by hundreds of students each week generates enough loans and searches that the time saved and improved retrieval justify the cost, provided a manual back-up is kept.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>This extends the 15-mark question with a how/where/what/when part and a full evaluation. In (b), each stage needs both sides and a contrast. In (c), check that all four questions are answered and that retrieval and transmission are not forgotten. In (d), reserve the top marks for answers that include a mitigation and justify the recommendation by the library's scale; a bare \"yes, because it is faster\" earns little. Students may quote their own filing-activity timings as evidence.</p>"}
