     "up": {"title":"Justify communication tools for a school (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: justify, Grade One extension (15 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Justify</b> ONE appropriate Internet or communication tool for EACH of the three needs (i), (ii) and (iii). For each, link a feature to the scenario and weigh one drawback. <i>(9 marks)</i></li><li>For each tool, <b>state</b> whether it is real-time or asynchronous and <b>explain</b> why that timing suits the need. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The principal suggests using one cross-platform messaging group for all three needs. <b>Evaluate</b> this suggestion. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":280,"max":520,"frame":"<p><b>(a)(i)</b> For urgent closure notices, the school should use ... because ... This means that ... A drawback is ..., however ...<br><b>(ii)</b> For meetings with the Barbados school, ... is appropriate because ... so that ... A drawback is ...<br><b>(iii)</b> For revision questions, a ... is best because ... A drawback is ...<br><b>(b)</b> Tool (i) is ..., which suits ... because ... Tool (ii) is ... Tool (iii) is ...<br><b>(c)</b> A single group would ... However, ... Therefore ...</p>","checklist":["I named one specific syllabus tool for each need.","Each justification links a feature to the scenario, including parents with basic phones.","I weighed a drawback for each tool and said how to manage it.","I classified each tool by timing AND explained why the timing suits the need.","In (c) I gave a point for and a point against the single group, and a judgement."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"(a)(i) Urgent notices","marks":3,"look":["sms|text messag","basic phone|any (mobile )?phone|no data|cellular|without (the )?internet","cost|160|character|short|per message|however"],"min":3,"strong":"SMS is justified by its fit to basic phones, and a drawback is weighed.","weak":"Choose SMS: it works on any phone over the cellular network without data, so every parent is reached; note the cost per message or the short length, and why it is acceptable here."},{"criterion":"(a)(ii) Meetings with Barbados","marks":3,"look":["web.?conferenc|zoom|teams|google meet|video (call|meeting)","real.?time|screen shar|face|travel|live","bandwidth|connection|freez|time|schedul|however"],"min":3,"strong":"Web conferencing is justified with a feature, and a drawback is managed.","weak":"Justify web conferencing by screen sharing and live video that save inter-island travel; weigh the need for a stable connection and how to manage it."},{"criterion":"(a)(iii) Revision questions","marks":3,"look":["forum|discussion board","archive|search|thread|future|stored|kept|later","moderat|wrong|accura|slow|spam|however"],"min":3,"strong":"The forum is justified by its archive, and moderation is addressed.","weak":"A discussion forum keeps threaded, searchable posts for future classes; it needs a teacher to moderate for accuracy and respect."},{"criterion":"(b) Timing classified and explained","marks":3,"look":["asynchronous","real.?time|synchronous","because|suits|so that|which means"],"min":3,"strong":"All three tools are classified, with a reason why each timing fits the need.","weak":"SMS is asynchronous, so parents receive it whenever their phone has signal; web conferencing is real-time, so decisions are made in the meeting; the forum is asynchronous, so students post and read when they study."},{"criterion":"(c) Evaluation of a single messaging group","marks":3,"look":["messaging|group|whatsapp","basic phone|data|search|archive|buried|noise|privacy|600","however|but|therefore|on balance|not suitable|recommend"],"min":3,"strong":"Both sides are weighed and a clear judgement is given.","weak":"A single group is free and familiar, but parents with basic phones cannot use it, answers are buried in a busy chat, and adding 600 students' families raises privacy concerns; conclude that separate tools fit the needs better."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)(i)</b> The school should send closure notices by <b>SMS</b>. SMS travels over the cellular network and works on any mobile phone, including basic phones, without a data plan or app, so every parent receives the notice within minutes even if their Internet is down. A drawback is that each message has a small cost and is limited to about 160 characters, but a short notice such as \"School closed Monday: flooding\" fits easily, and the cost is justified for urgent safety information.</p><p><b>(ii)</b> A <b>web-conferencing tool</b> such as Microsoft Teams is appropriate because it provides real-time video, audio and screen sharing, so teachers in Antriol and Barbados can discuss lessons face to face and look at the same slides without the cost of flights. A drawback is that it needs a stable connection; if video freezes, staff can switch it off and continue with audio, and the meeting can be recorded for anyone who drops out.</p><p><b>(iii)</b> A <b>discussion forum</b> is best because questions and answers are organised into threads by topic and stored permanently, so next year's students can search and read earlier explanations. A drawback is that wrong answers or disrespectful posts may appear, so a teacher should moderate the forum and mark correct answers.</p><p><b>(b)</b> SMS is <b>asynchronous</b>, which suits urgent notices because the message waits at the message centre and is delivered as soon as each parent's phone has signal. Web conferencing is <b>real-time</b>, which suits the monthly meetings because teachers can raise questions and agree plans on the spot. The forum is <b>asynchronous</b>, which suits revision because students can post and reply whenever they study, and the posts remain for later.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A single messaging group would be free, familiar and fast for parents who already use the app. However, parents with basic phones or no data could not receive closure notices, revision answers would be buried in a busy chat and hard to search, and putting hundreds of families into one group raises privacy concerns about phone numbers. On balance, the suggestion does not meet the needs: separate tools, each chosen for its feature, serve the school far better.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>In (a), each 3-mark justification needs the tool, a feature linked to the scenario and a weighed drawback; \"SMS is fast\" alone earns one mark. In (b), the timing must be explained in terms of the need, not just named. In (c), reserve full marks for answers that give both sides, including the basic-phone and privacy problems, before judging.</p>"}
