  "title": "Paper 02 practice: information after a hurricane",
  "genre": "Paper 02 structured response: data, information and knowledge, information characteristics, processing cycle, IT tools",
  "stimulus": "<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p><p class=\"passage\">The <b>Bayfront Relief Network</b> is a volunteer organisation in Roseau, Dominica. After a major hurricane, volunteers walk through Goodwill, Bath Estate and Pottersville with paper forms, recording each house number, roof damage (none, partial, total), the number of occupants and whether the home has electricity and water. Each evening the forms are carried to a church hall, where two volunteers type them into a laptop spreadsheet. The co-ordinator, Ms. Arrindell, then decides which districts receive tarpaulins, water and generators first. Last year many forms were lost or illegible, some were three days old before they were entered, and two districts received no supplies because their forms were never typed in. Mobile data is unreliable for the first few days, but SMS usually works.</p>",
  "prompt": "<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Using examples from the scenario, distinguish among <b>data</b>, <b>information</b> and <b>knowledge</b>. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain why <b>TWO</b> characteristics of information are critical to the co-ordinator's decisions. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Describe how an automated information processing cycle could handle the damage reports, referring to <b>input</b>, <b>processing</b>, <b>output</b>, <b>storage</b> and <b>feedback</b>. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Justify the use of <b>ONE</b> hardware tool and <b>TWO</b> communication tools that the network could use during the first week after the hurricane. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>",
  "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Data are ... e.g. ... Information is ... e.g. ... Knowledge is ... e.g. Ms. Arrindell uses ... to decide ...<br><b>(b)</b> Timeliness matters because ... Completeness/accuracy matters because ...<br><b>(c)</b> Input: volunteers capture ... Processing: the system sorts/calculates ... Output: ... Storage: ... Feedback: ...<br><b>(d)</b> Hardware: ... because ... Communication tool 1: SMS ... because ... Tool 2: ... because ...</p>",
  "checklist": [
   "I defined data, information and knowledge and gave a scenario example of each.",
   "I explained (not just named) two characteristics of information and linked each to a decision.",
   "I described all five stages of the processing cycle using the damage reports.",
   "I justified one hardware tool and two communication tools, using the mobile data problem.",
   "I labelled my answers (a) to (d)."
  ],
  "rubric": [
   {
    "criterion": "(a) Data, information and knowledge",
    "marks": 3,
    "look": [
     "raw|unprocessed|unorgani[sz]ed|house number|form",
     "processed|meaning|summar|total|list of|table",
     "knowledge|decid|decision|experience|insight"
    ],
    "min": 2,
    "strong": "Clear three-way distinction with scenario examples.",
    "weak": "Define each term and give an example: data (a single form entry), information (a summary of homes with total roof damage per district), knowledge (using that summary and experience to decide which district gets tarpaulins first)."
   },
   {
    "criterion": "(b) Characteristics of information",
    "marks": 4,
    "look": [
     "timel|out of date|three days|current|up to date",
     "complete|missing|lost|never typed",
     "accura|reliab|illegible|error",
     "because|so that|as a result|which means"
    ],
    "min": 2,
    "strong": "You explained why each characteristic affects the decisions.",
    "weak": "Name the characteristic and show its effect: \"Timeliness: forms three days old mean supplies go to homes whose needs have changed, so people wait longer for water.\""
   },
   {
    "criterion": "(c) Automated processing cycle",
    "marks": 5,
    "look": [
     "input|captur|enter|tablet|phone|form",
     "process|sort|calculat|total|analys|rank|priorit",
     "output|report|map|chart|dashboard|list",
     "stor|database|cloud|backup|retriev",
     "feedback|evaluat|updat|re-?survey|loop"
    ],
    "min": 4,
    "strong": "All five stages described in context.",
    "weak": "Describe each stage in the scenario, e.g. \"Feedback: after supplies are delivered, volunteers update each home's status, which loops back as new input.\""
   },
   {
    "criterion": "(d) Justifying IT tools",
    "marks": 3,
    "look": [
     "laptop|tablet|smartphone|generator|power bank|modem|router|satellite|gps|printer",
     "sms|text messag",
     "voip|whatsapp|radio|social media|web.?conferenc|mailing list|messaging|phone call"
    ],
    "min": 2,
    "strong": "Tools are justified with reasons tied to the scenario.",
    "weak": "Give a reason for each tool: \"SMS, because it works on the basic mobile network when mobile data is down, so volunteers can send each district's totals the same day.\""
   }
  ],
  "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Data</b> are raw, unprocessed facts that have no meaning on their own, such as a single form entry \"House 14, roof: total, occupants: 5, electricity: no\". <b>Information</b> is data that has been processed so that it has meaning, for example a table showing that Goodwill has 38 homes with total roof damage and 120 people without water. <b>Knowledge</b> is the use of information, together with experience, to make decisions: Ms. Arrindell knows that homes with total roof damage and young children must be served first, so she sends tarpaulins to Goodwill before Pottersville.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Timeliness:</b> information must be available while it is still useful. Forms that are three days old describe needs that have already changed, so supplies may go to homes that have been helped while others wait, which wastes scarce resources. <b>Completeness:</b> information must include all the relevant data. Because two districts' forms were never typed in, the co-ordinator's summary was incomplete and those districts received no supplies at all. Incomplete information leads to unfair and wrong decisions.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Input:</b> volunteers capture data directly on a smartphone or tablet form with drop-down choices (none, partial, total), which validates entries and removes illegible handwriting. <b>Processing:</b> the system sorts and collates reports by district and calculates totals, such as homes without water, and ranks districts by need. <b>Output:</b> the co-ordinator receives a priority list, chart or map each morning showing which districts need which supplies. <b>Storage:</b> reports are stored in a database (with a cloud backup) so they can be retrieved, compared over days and not lost. <b>Feedback:</b> after deliveries, volunteers update each home's status; this evaluation loops back to the input stage so the next day's priorities reflect what has already been done.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Hardware:</b> rugged tablets or smartphones with power banks, because volunteers can enter data at the doorstep and devices can be charged without mains electricity. <b>SMS:</b> text messages work on the basic mobile network when mobile data fails, so team leaders can send district totals and receive instructions immediately. <b>VoIP or cross-platform messaging (e.g. WhatsApp) once data returns:</b> volunteers can share photos of damage and voice notes in a group at little cost, which speeds up co-ordination; its disadvantage is that it depends on Internet access.</p>",
  "teacherTip": "<p>Common errors: defining knowledge as \"a lot of information\" (it is the <b>use</b> of information for decision making); listing characteristics such as \"timeliness, accuracy\" without explaining the effect on the decision; naming the IPOS stages without describing them in the scenario (CXC caps a stated-only list); forgetting <b>feedback</b>. For \"justify\", every tool needs a reason linked to the scenario (power cuts, no mobile data).</p>",
